condathis data directory. There micromamba keeps its repodata cache,
instead of in \verb{~/.cache/conda}, and the \code{mamba/proc} directory of
\verb{micromamba run}. The scripts that packages run when they are
installed or removed see this value too. The \code{activate.d} scripts,
while the activation is recorded, and the commands of \code{\link[=run]{run()}},
\code{\link[=run_bin]{run_bin()}} and \code{\link[=run_pipeline]{run_pipeline()}} keep the session's value.
}
\item{\code{HOME} (\code{USERPROFILE} on Windows)}{
For \verb{micromamba env list}, \verb{micromamba env remove} and, unless it
needs your home directory, \verb{micromamba create}: the \verb{home/}
directory of the condathis data directory. So \code{micromamba} does not
rewrite \verb{~/.conda/environments.txt} (see Details in
\code{\link[=get_install_dir]{get_install_dir()}}). The scripts that packages run when they are
installed or removed by these commands see this value too. On
Windows, micromamba looks there for the Start Menu shortcuts of the
removed packages.
}
}

Python, pip and uv: the Python state of the R session never reaches a
process of \code{condathis}. So the python of an environment finds only its
own modules, and \verb{micromamba create} installs the \verb{pip:} section of an
environment file into the environment, never elsewhere. For every
process, \code{micromamba} itself and the commands of \code{\link[=run]{run()}}, \code{\link[=run_bin]{run_bin()}}
and \code{\link[=run_pipeline]{run_pipeline()}}, with and without activation:

\describe{
\item{\code{PYTHONHOME}, \code{PYTHONPATH}, \code{PYTHONUSERBASE}, \code{PYTHONSTARTUP}}{
Removed. To give a command a \code{PYTHONPATH}, set it in the command,
such as \code{run("env", "PYTHONPATH=/my/modules", "python", "script.py")}
on Linux and macOS, or add the directory to \code{sys.path} in the
script.
}
\item{\code{PYTHONNOUSERSITE}}{
Set to \code{"1"}: the user's site directory (\verb{~/.local/lib/python3.*},
on Windows a \code{Python} directory in \code{APPDATA}) is not on \code{sys.path}.
}
\item{\code{UV_PYTHON}, \code{UV_SYSTEM_PYTHON}, \code{UV_PROJECT_ENVIRONMENT}}{
Removed, so \code{uv} uses the python of the environment.
}
\item{\code{PIP_USER}, \code{PIP_REQUIRE_VIRTUALENV}}{
Set to \code{"false"}, which overrides \code{user} and \code{require-virtualenv} in
a pip configuration file; \code{PIP_REQUIRE_VENV}, an alias that pip
also reads, is removed. Otherwise pip installed into the user's
site directory, or failed: the user's site directory is off, and a
Conda environment is not a virtual environment. So
\code{run("python", "-m", "pip", "install", ...)} installs into the
environment.
}
\item{\code{PIP_TARGET}, \code{PIP_PREFIX}, \code{PIP_ROOT}}{
Removed, whatever their value. A \code{target}, \code{prefix} or \code{root} in a
pip configuration file still applies (an environment variable
cannot unset it). To install elsewhere, pass \code{--target} or
\code{--prefix} to pip in the command.
}
}

An activated virtual environment of the session (\code{VIRTUAL_ENV}) is
undone as well (see \emph{The activation of the R session} below). The
activation of the environment of a command comes after all this, so a
variable that an \code{activate.d} script sets reaches the command.

For \verb{micromamba create} and \verb{micromamba install}, which run pip, or uv
when the environment file lists it, for the \verb{pip:} section,
\code{VIRTUAL_ENV} and \code{VIRTUAL_ENV_PROMPT} are also removed, whatever their
value.

For \verb{micromamba list}, which \code{\link[=list_packages]{list_packages()}} runs, and which runs
\verb{python -m pip inspect} in the environment: \code{PIP_CONFIG_FILE} is the
null device (\verb{/dev/null}, on Windows \code{nul}), so pip reads no
configuration file, \code{PIP_USER} is \code{"false"}, \code{PIP_PATH} and \code{PIP_LOCAL}
are removed, and the working directory is a temporary directory, not the
one of R. pip then reports only the packages of the environment.

Every other \verb{PIP_*} and \verb{UV_*} variable reaches pip and uv unchanged.

\emph{The activation of the R session.} R can run in an activated Conda or
pixi environment, or in several nested ones: R installed in a Conda
environment, R started from a shell where \verb{conda activate} or
\verb{pixi shell} ran, or R started through a pixi global binary. The
processes that \code{condathis} starts do not get that activation, within
the limits below. So a program of R's own environment does not run in
place of one that the environment of the command lacks. Before the
overrides above,
\code{condathis} changes the copy of the session's environment this way:

\describe{
\item{\code{PATH}}{
The binary directories of these Conda prefixes are removed: the
prefixes of \code{CONDA_PREFIX}, of every \verb{CONDA_PREFIX_<n>} and
\verb{CONDA_ENV_SHLVL_<n>_CONDA_PREFIX}, and the prefix that R itself is
installed in. Only a directory with a \code{conda-meta} directory counts
as a prefix. Other entries stay, such as
the \code{condabin} directory of \verb{conda init} and \verb{~/.pixi/bin}. One
exception on Windows: a command that is a program of R's own prefix,
such as \code{file.path(R.home("bin"), "Rscript.exe")}, gets the binary
directories of that prefix back at the end of its \code{PATH}, because it
loads its DLLs from there.
}
\item{\verb{CONDA_PREFIX_<n>}, \verb{CONDA_ENV_SHLVL_<n>_*}, \code{CONDA_EXE},
\code{CONDA_PYTHON_EXE}, \verb{_CE_CONDA}, \verb{_CE_M}, \code{MAMBA_EXE}}{
Removed. These variables record the activation of the session.
}
\item{\verb{PIXI_PROJECT_*}, \verb{PIXI_WORKSPACE_*}, \verb{PIXI_ENVIRONMENT_*},
\code{PIXI_PROMPT}, \code{PIXI_IN_SHELL}, \code{PIXI_EXE}}{
Removed. Other \verb{PIXI_*} variables, such as \code{PIXI_HOME}, stay.
}
\item{\verb{CONDA_BACKUP_<X>}, \verb{<X>_CONDA_BACKUP}, \verb{CONDA_ENV_SHLVL_<n>_<X>}}{
The value of \verb{<X>} before the activation, saved by the activation
scripts of compiler and other packages, or by pixi. \verb{<X>} gets that
value back, as the deactivation scripts do: an empty backup of the
first two forms removes \verb{<X>}. The backups are removed.
}
\item{Variables of \code{conda-meta/state} and \code{etc/conda/env_vars.d}}{
A variable that these files of the prefixes above set is removed
when the session has exactly that value.
}
\item{Variables of a pixi global binary}{
When one of the prefixes above is an environment of \verb{pixi global},
a variable that its binaries set when they start is removed when the
session has exactly that value. pixi records them in
\code{bin/trampoline_configuration} of its home. R started through such a
binary gets the whole activation of its environment, such as the
compiler variables \code{CC}, \code{CFLAGS} and \code{CONDA_BUILD_SYSROOT}, and a
compiler of the environment of a command would see them.
}
\item{\code{VIRTUAL_ENV}, \code{VIRTUAL_ENV_PROMPT}}{
When \code{VIRTUAL_ENV} names a Python virtual environment (a directory
with \code{pyvenv.cfg}), both are removed, and its \code{bin} directory (on
Windows \code{Scripts}) leaves \code{PATH}. Activating a virtual environment
sets them, and so does reticulate inside the R session when it uses
one, also through \code{Sys.setenv()}.
}
\item{What reticulate changed for its python}{
When reticulate has started Python in the R session, the \code{lib}
directories that it put in front of \code{LD_LIBRARY_PATH} on Linux (of
its python and of the python a virtual environment was made from)
are removed. For a python of a Conda environment, reticulate puts the
binary directories of that environment in front of \code{PATH}; they are
removed as those of the prefixes above.
}
}

Some changes cannot be undone. A value that an \code{activate.d} script of
the session's environment set stays, unless it saved a backup above or
a pixi global binary recorded it, and so does a \code{PATH} entry such a
script added. For example, R installed in a Conda environment that has
the compiler packages, and started from a shell where that environment
was activated, passes \code{CC}, \code{CFLAGS}, \code{CONDA_BUILD_SYSROOT} and the
other compiler variables of that environment to the processes it
starts. The binary directory of an environment that no variable names
any more stays too, such as the one of a pixi global binary that started
the shell before another one replaced its \code{CONDA_PREFIX}. reticulate
moves the directory of a python that is neither in a virtual nor in a
Conda environment, such as \verb{/usr/bin}, to the front of \code{PATH}; it stays
there. On macOS, when \verb{/usr/local/opt/openblas} exists, loading
reticulate sets \code{OPENBLAS} and, when it is unset, \code{OMP_NUM_THREADS=1};
they stay too.

\emph{The startup of R.} R also changes its own environment when it starts,
and the processes that \code{condathis} starts do not get those changes
either. So an R of the environment of the command never loads packages
built for the R of the session, and a program of the environment never
loads R's libraries or certificates:

\describe{
\item{\verb{R_*}, \verb{_R_*}}{
Removed, also a variable the user set, such as \code{R_LIBS_USER} or
\code{R_PROFILE_USER}: only R reads them, and an R that the command
starts sets its own. The R of the session keeps them.
}
\item{\code{EDITOR}, \code{PAGER}, \code{MAKE}, \code{SED}, \code{TAR}, \code{LN_S},
\code{CURL_CA_BUNDLE}, \code{MSYS2_ENV_CONV_EXCL}}{
Removed when the session has exactly the value that R's startup
gives them (\code{etc/Renviron} of R). A value the user set stays, such
as \code{EDITOR=nvim}.
}
\item{\code{LD_LIBRARY_PATH}, \code{DYLD_FALLBACK_LIBRARY_PATH},
\code{DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH}}{
The directories that R put in front (\code{etc/ldpaths} of R) are
removed. The directories after them stay.
}
\item{\code{PATH} on Windows}{
The Rtools directories that R put in front (\code{R_RTOOLS45_PATH}) are
removed. A command finds Rtools only when the \code{PATH} that R started
with has it.
}
}

\code{condathis} learns the values of R's startup by starting R's own
\code{Rscript} once per R session, without the user's \code{.Renviron} and
\code{.Rprofile}. When that fails, only the \verb{R_*} variables and the Rtools
directories are removed.

With the default \code{activate = TRUE} of \code{\link[=run]{run()}}, \code{\link[=run_bin]{run_bin()}} and
\code{\link[=run_pipeline]{run_pipeline()}}, \code{condathis} applies activation variables on top. For
the micromamba backend, these are the variables that a real
\verb{micromamba run} activation sets. They include the variables from
\code{activate.d} scripts shipped by the installed packages. These scripts run
with the command's \code{TMPDIR}, user cache and working directory.
\code{condathis} records the variables once per environment and R session. It
reuses them until the environment (its packages, its \code{activate.d} scripts
or \code{env_vars.d} files) or the session's environment variables change. It
starts the command itself. Values that name the command's \code{TMPDIR} are
recorded again for every command. Values that name the working directory
are recorded again in another working directory. For other backends, the
backend package sets the activation variables (see \code{\link[=register_backend]{register_backend()}}).
When the \code{PATH} of an activation names none of the environment's binary
directories, \code{condathis} puts them in front of it.

In both modes and for every backend, a command of \code{\link[=run]{run()}}, \code{\link[=run_bin]{run_bin()}}
and \code{\link[=run_pipeline]{run_pipeline()}} runs in exactly one activated environment,
whatever the R session runs in: \code{CONDA_SHLVL} and \code{MAMBA_SHLVL} are
\code{"1"}, and \code{CONDA_PROMPT_MODIFIER} and \code{MAMBA_PROMPT_MODIFIER} are
\code{""}, whatever the activation set.

With \code{activate = FALSE}, \code{\link[=run]{run()}}, \code{\link[=run_bin]{run_bin()}} and \code{\link[=run_pipeline]{run_pipeline()}} set
this fixed set instead, without any backend code and without the
\code{activate.d} scripts and \code{env_vars.d} files of the packages:

\describe{
\item{\code{CONDA_PREFIX}, \code{CONDA_DEFAULT_ENV}}{
The environment directory and its name. For an environment given by
\code{\link[=env_path]{env_path()}}, the name is its directory.
}
\item{\code{CONDA_SHLVL}, \code{MAMBA_SHLVL}}{
Set to \code{"1"}.
}
\item{\code{CONDA_PROMPT_MODIFIER}, \code{MAMBA_PROMPT_MODIFIER}}{
Set to \code{""}.
}
\item{\code{PATH}}{
The environment's binary directories, in front of the \code{PATH}
described above.
}
}

\code{TMPDIR} and \code{CONDA_ENVS_PATH} are the same as with \code{activate = TRUE}:
they come from the clean variables above.

On Windows, variable names are matched case-insensitively. An override
replaces any inherited spelling of the same name, such as \code{Path}.
}

\section{micromamba discovery}{


\code{condathis} uses the first candidate that exists and reports a
compatible version:
\enumerate{
\item The \code{condathis.micromamba_path} option.
\item The \code{CONDATHIS_MICROMAMBA_PATH} environment variable.
\item The binary managed by \code{condathis}, see \code{\link[=micromamba_bin_path]{micromamba_bin_path()}}.
\item A \code{micromamba} next to the running R, when R is installed in a Conda
environment.
\item A \code{micromamba} inside \code{CONDA_PREFIX}.
\item A \code{micromamba} in the \code{micromamba-env} environment of the micromamba
backend, in the \code{envs} directory of the condathis data directory.
\code{condathis} ignores an environment of that name that another backend
created.
\item A \code{micromamba} on \code{PATH}.
}

A candidate that is a binary exposed by \verb{pixi global} (a trampoline,
with a \verb{trampoline_configuration/<name>.json} file next to it) is
replaced by the binary that it starts, the \code{exe} of that file, so the
activation of the pixi environment that the trampoline sets never
reaches \code{micromamba}. The option and the variable keep the value you
set.

A compatible version is 2.8.1 or newer. When no candidate is
compatible, the first function that needs \code{micromamba} calls
\code{\link[=install_micromamba]{install_micromamba()}}, which downloads the default version into the
managed location. That function says so first, unless its \code{verbose} is
\code{"silent"}, with a message of class \code{condathis_micromamba_downloading}.
Queries, such as \code{\link[=list_envs]{list_envs()}}, never download it.
}

\section{Interactive sessions}{


In interactive sessions, \code{condathis} shows spinners for every \code{verbose}
level except \code{"silent"}. A session is interactive when
\code{\link[rlang:is_interactive]{rlang::is_interactive()}} returns \code{TRUE}. \code{\link[=install_micromamba]{install_micromamba()}} shows
the progress of its download only in interactive sessions, and only
when its \code{verbose} is \code{"output"} or \code{"full"}. When another function
installs micromamba because no usable binary was found, the progress
shows only with \code{verbose = "full"}. Set the \code{rlang_interactive} option
to \code{FALSE} to turn spinners and progress off in an interactive session.
}

